course: dictionary
lesson: 4926
title: Fair and not taking sides
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Impartial means fair to everyone and not taking sides, so you do not favour one person or team. A referee who gives the same penalties to both teams is being impartial.
explain: Look for clues in the sentence. «Bias means leaning towards one side and impartial means taking no side.» Bias is leaning towards one side, and impartial is the opposite. When two words are set against each other, the contrast shows you the meaning.
explain: You will hear impartial in phrases like «an impartial referee», «impartial advice» and «stay impartial». In a court, a judge who is impartial decides only on the facts, not because they like or dislike someone.
explain: Check it by swapping in your meaning. Does «an impartial referee» still make sense as «a fair referee who does not take sides»? If it does, you have understood the word.
fact: Impartial is an adjective. The noun is impartiality, and the adverb is impartially.
fact: «Impartial» means fair to everyone and not taking sides.
fact: «Bias» means leaning towards one side, so it is the opposite of impartial.
fact: In law, an impartial judge or jury decides only on the facts, without friendship or dislike affecting the decision.
fact: Impartial advice weighs up both sides before it gives an opinion.
exercise: «Bias means leaning towards one side and impartial means taking no side.» What does impartial mean? | Leaning towards one team | Loud and angry | *Fair, and not taking sides | Very old
exercise: Find and copy the word in the sentence that means leaning towards one side. | impartial | taking | *bias | means
exercise: A referee gives the same penalties to both teams for the same fouls. What is the referee being? | *impartial | famous | tired | biased
test: Which of these is an example of impartial advice? | *Advice that weighs both sides fairly | Advice given only to the loudest person | Advice that is never explained | Advice that favours your best friend
test: A judge who is impartial decides only on the facts. Which thing must NOT affect the decision? | The clock on the wall | *Friendship or dislike | Facts | The evidence in the case
exam: Why is it important for the news to be impartial? | It stops anyone from watching | *Viewers hear both sides fairly and can decide for themselves | It makes the news shorter | It means the news is always happy
